<a href="https://x.com/gilg_oscar"><Icon name="mdi:twitter" aria-hidden="true" />Twitter</a>
</nav>
</div>
</header>
<section aria-label="Research and writing">
<p>Some papers/posts I like related to this:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.06390">Automated alignment is harder than you think</a></li>
<li><a href="https://www.redwoodresearch.org/blog/a-basic-case-for-accelerating-conceptual">A basic case for accelerating conceptual reasoning capabilities</a></li>
<li><a href="https://joecarlsmith.com/2025/03/14/ai-for-ai-safety/">AI for AI safety — Joe Carlsmith</a></li>
<li><a href="https://vitalik.eth.limo/general/2025/01/05/dacc2.html">d/acc: one year later — Vitalik Buterin</a></li>
</ul>
<h2>Projects</h2>
<div class="project-heading"><h3><a href="https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/WbmAqtfkrCqRHxAGh/commentbench-can-models-match-human-comments-on-ai-safety">CommentBench</a></h3><span class="date">September 2026</span></div>
<ul>
<li>We measure how well model-generated comments match human comments on conceptual AI-safety posts, drafts and shortforms. Models write comments, and we measure how many of the substantive human points they recover. This work continued with <a href="https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/gAnqGRgQ2bQ4jcur6/redwoodbench-can-frontier-models-imitate-conceptual-ai">RedwoodBench</a>, led by Hasan Baig at Redwood Research.</li>
</ul>
<div class="project-heading"><h3><a href="https://github.com/hamzah2304/messageboardauditbench">SlopvestigatorBench</a></h3><span class="date">September 2026</span></div>
<ul>
<li>Can an AI agent investigate raw logs of AI agents misbehaving and find what human investigators found? We turn three real incidents into benchmarks: agents get the logs, investigate under a time budget, and write reports that we score against human findings.</li>
</ul>
<p>Blog post: <a href="https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/wt4kk6vFPEhkXvF8Q/how-good-are-slop-vestigators">How good are slop-vestigators?</a></p>
<div class="project-heading"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.13339">Probing Persona-Dependent Preferences in Language Models</a></h3><span class="date">May 2026</span></div>
<p class="context">MATS 9.0 · Accepted at NeurIPS 2026</p>
<ul>
<li>We found a shared preference direction that predicts which tasks models choose across different personas. With Patrick Butlin (<a href="https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/pxC2RAeoBrvK8ivMf/models-have-linear-representations-of-what-tasks-they-like-1">post</a>, <a href="https://github.com/oscar-gilg/Preferences">code</a>, <a href="https://x.com/gilg_oscar/status/2056387363840893075">thread</a>).</li>
</ul>
<div class="project-heading"><h3><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.05532">Split Personality Training</a></h3><span class="date">February 2026</span></div>
<p class="context">SPAR</p>
<ul>
<li>Trigger-conditioned persona fine-tuning. Accepted at ICML 2026.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Other work</h2>
<div class="project-heading"><h3><a href="https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/4pgGkbwvdmxKPcJsM/bench-on-the-clocktower">Bench on the Clocktower</a></h3><span class="date">September 2026</span></div>
<ul>
<li>We turn Blood on the Clocktower into a multi-agent benchmark, and a testbed for deception and coordination capabilities. Agents sometimes coordinate sophisticated lies when playing Evil, but tend to fall for them when playing Good.</li>
</ul>
<div class="project-heading"><h3><a href="https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/ht8uMbAByDCKWHa9H/ai-welfare-research-needs-basic-science">AI welfare research needs basic science</a></h3><span class="date">July 2026</span></div>
<ul>
<li>We argue for iterative, bottom-up basic science in AI welfare research. Current theories were calibrated to humans and may not generalise to AI. Empirical work should help us refine those theories without presupposing which one is right.</li>
</ul>
<div class="project-heading"><h3><a href="https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/5gDjg4oN4kJKMLovs/ablating-split-personality-training">Ablating Split Personality Training</a></h3><span class="date">March 2026</span></div>
<ul>
<li>I ablated the key components of Split-Personality training to understand which parts actually matter.</li>
</ul>
</section>
</main>
<style>
:global(*) { box-sizing: border-box; }
:global(html) { background: #f8f6f1; color: #29332f; font: 16px/1.65 Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; }
:global(body) { margin: 0; }
main { max-width: 920px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 64px 32px 80px; }
a { color: #356c64; text-underline-offset: 3px; text-decoration-thickness: 1px; }
a:hover { color: #224c45; }
a:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid #356c64; outline-offset: 4px; }
.intro { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 260px; gap: 44px; align-items: start; margin-bottom: 32px; }
h1 { margin: 0 0 24px; font-size: 40px; line-height: 1.15; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: -1px; }
p { margin: 12px 0; }
.intro-copy p { margin: 0 0 18px; }
.portrait-links { text-align: center; padding-top: 4px; }
.portrait { width: 220px; height: 220px; object-fit: cover; border-radius: 50%; }
nav { display: flex; justify-content: center; gap: 16px; margin-top: 16px; }
nav a { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 5px; text-decoration: none; white-space: nowrap; }
nav :global(svg) { width: 18px; height: 18px; }
h2 { margin: 40px 0 22px; border-top: 1px solid #d9ddd4; padding-top: 14px; font-size: 1rem; font-weight: 600; }
h3 { margin: 0; font-size: 1rem; font-weight: 600; }
.project-heading { display: flex; align-items: baseline; justify-content: space-between; gap: 24px; margin-top: 26px; }
.date { color: #626b62; white-space: nowrap; }
.context { color: #626b62; margin: 4px 0 10px; }
ul { margin: 12px 0 20px; padding-left: 24px; }
li { padding-left: 2px; margin-bottom: 10px; }
.skip { position: absolute; top: -60px; left: 16px; padding: 8px; background: #f8f6f1; }
.skip:focus { top: 8px; }
@media (max-width: 720px) {
 main { padding: 40px 24px 60px; }
 .intro { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 220px; gap: 24px; }
 .portrait { width: 180px; height: 180px; }
 nav { gap: 10px; }
}
@media (max-width: 600px) {
 .intro { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px; }
 .intro-copy { display: contents; }
 .intro-copy p { order: 2; }
 .portrait-links { align-self: center; order: 1; margin-bottom: 16px; }
 h1 { font-size: 36px; }
 .project-heading { flex-direction: column; gap: 2px; }
}
</style>
</body>
</html>
